WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.560
Ian McKellen was one of the most&nbsp;
recognizable actors of the early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.920
having instantly cemented himself as a geek&nbsp;
icon with his performances as Gandalf the Grey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:16.080
and Magneto. Today, though, he's not so much of a&nbsp;
presence in Hollywood. So just where has he gone?

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.480
When actors are young, they tend to be hungry&nbsp;
for creative and professional validation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.040
and often have to work incredibly hard to&nbsp;
establish their career. When actors are older,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.600
however - or more comfortable financially&nbsp;
- they usually have different priorities.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.480
Considering the number of major films Ian McKellen&nbsp;
has starred in over the past 20 years alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.000
it's probably safe to assume that&nbsp;
he's pretty financially well-off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.560
As such, he doesn't have to take every part&nbsp;
offered to him. In fact, if he doesn't want to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.240
he probably doesn't have to take any parts at all,&nbsp;
and can simply spend his time however he sees fit.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.960
It's also worth pointing out that McKellen is&nbsp;
now more than 80 years old. Working in Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.280
isn't easy even when you're young, and it's&nbsp;
entirely possible that the rigors of film and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.480
television production are simply more difficult&nbsp;
for him than they might have been 20 years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.680
Nobody can say for sure, of course,&nbsp;
but it's definitely not impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.680
that McKellen neither wants nor needs to&nbsp;
be working quite as much as he used to.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.640
As a senior citizen with a history of&nbsp;
prostate cancer, Ian McKellen is part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.120
of a segment of the population that is&nbsp;
more susceptible than most to COVID-19.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:25.120
So if McKellen seemed like he kept a low profile&nbsp;
during much of 2020, he had a pretty good reason.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.600
Thankfully, McKellen received his first&nbsp;
shot of the COVID vaccine in December 2020.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.880
As a senior citizen, McKellen was among the first&nbsp;
in line for a first dose of the vaccination.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:41.440
"I feel very special and it's a very&nbsp;
happy day, this, I'm a bit euphoric."

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.560
In a press release issued shortly&nbsp;
afterwards, McKellen said:

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.360
"I feel honored to have received the COVID-19&nbsp;
vaccine and I would urge anyone who is offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.840
the vaccine to take up the offer [...] I really&nbsp;
hope that, as more people get vaccinated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.440
we will move further along the path&nbsp;
back to a more normal way of life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.480
particularly for the arts which&nbsp;
have suffered so much this year."

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.520
Infinitum: Subject Unknown is the kind of film&nbsp;
that could only have been produced during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.800
2020 lockdown. It's a time-loop story in&nbsp;
the vein of Groundhog Day and Palm Springs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.640
shot with just an iPhone and almost&nbsp;
solely carried by Tori Butler-Hart.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.600
So far, so unremarkable, right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.400
Infinitum does have at least one surprising&nbsp;
thing going for it - a cameo by Sir Ian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.040
McKellen. His character largely appears&nbsp;
in some in-universe documentary footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.160
which conveniently allowed McKellen to shoot his&nbsp;
role from the comfort and safety of his own home.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.880
So there you have it - if anyone tells you Ian&nbsp;
McKellen spent his lockdown twiddling his thumbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.120
Infinitum is evidence to the contrary.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.240
Although Ian McKellen's film and television&nbsp;
acting career began in the mid-1960s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.400
if you ask the average movie fan the one word&nbsp;
that pops to mind when they hear his name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.560
odds are they'll answer, "Gandalf."

00:02:51.127 --> 00:02:54.487
"You shall not pass!"

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.080
Peter Jackson's films based on the works of J.R.R.&nbsp;
Tolkien - and especially The Lord of the Rings -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.280
left a long-lasting impression on blockbuster&nbsp;
cinema and pop culture. Indeed, it seems fair to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.440
suggest that a number of the most successful&nbsp;
fantasy properties of the last two decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.920
would never have seen the light of day were it&nbsp;
not for the success of The Lord of the Rings.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.440
As the wizard Gandalf the Grey,&nbsp;
and later Gandalf the White,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.080
McKellen serves as a kind of mentor to the&nbsp;
story's protagonists. He also features in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.880
some of the trilogy's most iconic scenes -&nbsp;
including the battle against the Balrog of Moria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.880
the defense of Minas Tirith, and the&nbsp;
last stand at the gates of Mordor.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.800
Of course, those movies came out 20&nbsp;
years ago, and the onscreen adventures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.000
of Gandalf finally came to a close with&nbsp;
The Battle of the Five Armies back in 2014.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.200
Since McKellen's signature role&nbsp;
has effectively been exhausted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.600
he simply isn't going to be as visible in&nbsp;
the pop culture zeitgeist as he once was.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.160
Any comic book movie fan will tell you that&nbsp;
it's very rare indeed for a character to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.120
solely associated with just one actor. It was&nbsp;
only inevitable, then, that the legendary X-Men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.240
villain Magneto would carry on his big-screen&nbsp;
adventures with an actor other than Ian McKellen.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.520
After recognizing the failure of 2006's X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.120
The Last Stand and the limitations&nbsp;
of a gradually ageing cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.880
Fox decided to give their X-Men universe a&nbsp;
soft reboot in 2011, with the prequel X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:22.160
First Class. For this movie, Michael Fassbender&nbsp;
officially took over the role of Erik Lehnsherr.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:27.360
Fassbender played Magneto for eight years, until&nbsp;
a corporate buyout necessitated that X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:33.040
Dark Phoenix be the final installment in Fox's&nbsp;
X-Men universe. A time travel-oriented storyline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.760
allowed McKellen a brief return in&nbsp;
2014's X-Men: Days of Future Past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.880
but for all intents and purposes, he gave up&nbsp;
the role of Magneto a long time ago. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.920
with Marvel Studios moving forward with their own&nbsp;
version of the X-Men sometime in the near future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.880
you probably shouldn't expect to see McKellen&nbsp;
reprise his other iconic role any time soon.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.160
Since movies only tend to&nbsp;
run for a couple of hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.640
it can be easy to forget just how much time and&nbsp;
effort goes into producing and promoting them.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.880
To put it in perspective, films with the&nbsp;
budget and scope of, say, an X-Men movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.680
generally require about six months to shoot.&nbsp;
Once you throw in all the contractually-obligated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.080
promotional appearances typically required&nbsp;
of a starring actor, you can probably figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.240
that the cast have to devote a good year&nbsp;
of their lives to any single project. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.240
if you add up the three movies in the&nbsp;
X-Men trilogy, plus Days of Future Past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.960
it appears that Ian McKellen has dedicated&nbsp;
around four years to playing a single character.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:30.320
And The Lord of the Rings required&nbsp;
an even greater commitment than that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.400
As McKellen recently told GQ:

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.520
"I think [...] some of the actors who would&nbsp;
have been offered it before me were rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.960
put off by the idea of having to&nbsp;
live in New Zealand for a year."

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.920
Again, you've got all that time shooting, plus&nbsp;
the promo tours, all stretching from 2001 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.600
2003 and beyond - and then he did the whole thing&nbsp;
all over again with The Hobbit a decade later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.480
If anybody's earned a break from starring in&nbsp;
tentpole blockbuster franchises, it's this guy.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.560
As you may have noticed, it's not the&nbsp;
mid-'00s anymore - which means that Ian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.280
McKellen's tenure as one of the world's most&nbsp;
visible movie stars has probably come and gone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.840
But that doesn't mean he&nbsp;
hasn't been getting any work.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.920
First, of course, you've got Cats. Along&nbsp;
with a huge cast of fellow A-listers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.600
McKellen shows up in the widely and colorfully&nbsp;
criticized adaptation of the hit stage musical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.160
And while it definitely isn't enough to save&nbsp;
the film, his performance as Gus the Theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.280
Cat is guaranteed to delight fans of the&nbsp;
musical, young kids, and stoners alike.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:30.480
McKellen also appears in 2019's The Good&nbsp;
Liar, a modestly-budgeted con artist thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.360
directed by Bill Condon, also starring&nbsp;
Helen Mirren. Though hardly a smash hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.520
The Good Liar racked up a 63 percent&nbsp;
fresh Rotten Tomatoes rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:44.080
which means, at the very least, that critics liked&nbsp;
it a heck of a lot more than Cats. In the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.800
McKellen plays a grifter who attempts to liberate&nbsp;
a large sum of money from Mirren's character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:53.840
via a disingenuous romance. And sure, it's not&nbsp;
the best movie in the world, but the two stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.320
at least seem to be at the top of their game. As&nbsp;
The Observer noted in its review of the movie:

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.560
"The way they listen, look into each other’s&nbsp;
eyes, absorb each word and every silence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.600
while sipping a flute of champagne or sharing a&nbsp;
cup of tea - it’s a revelation, and a master class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.040
in acting at the Royal Academy of Dramatic Art at&nbsp;
the same time. Together, they redefine rapture."

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.880
While most people associate Ian&nbsp;
McKellen with his acting roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.640
for a few decades now he has also been a leading&nbsp;
activist in the UK's LGBT community. Back in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:29.200
late 1980s, the Section 28 law made discussing&nbsp;
homosexuality in schools illegal, and McKellen - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:34.560
famous U.K. actor even before his later hit&nbsp;
movies - came out in the media in protest.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.480
"I've never met a gay person&nbsp;
who regrets coming out."

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.000
Since then, he has co-founded the&nbsp;
Stonewall charity organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:48.000
and now serves as a patron for multiple other&nbsp;
LGBT rights groups. In his interview with GQ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.680
McKellen spoke about his ongoing&nbsp;
activism, telling the publication:

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.840
"I do hear people say, 'When I was growing up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:57.120
there were never any transgender&nbsp;
people about.' Well, there were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.200
you just didn't notice them. And that was&nbsp;
exactly what they used to say about gay people."

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.720
When an actor who was once prevalent in Hollywood&nbsp;
makes fewer and fewer appearances in movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.880
you might assume they're having difficulty getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.280
work. Sometimes that's true, of course,&nbsp;
but in this case, you'd be way off base.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:17.440
For McKellen, recent years have seen him&nbsp;
spending more time than ever on the stage.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.720
For example, between 2015 and 2020, McKellen&nbsp;
played the lead role in multiple productions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:27.360
of King Lear, co-starred in a production&nbsp;
of No Man's Land alongside Patrick Stewart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.000
and even pulled off a spoken word comedy show.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.800
While American moviegoers might think Ian&nbsp;
McKellen begins and ends with Gandalf and Magneto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.320
it's quite a different story on the&nbsp;
other side of the pond. In the UK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.360
he's more like an actor who&nbsp;
begins and ends with the theater…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.480
and makes a little room for Gandalf&nbsp;
and Magneto, every now and then.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.920
Hamlet Revenant was first announced back in&nbsp;
2017, and it doesn't appear as though a ton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.400
of progress has been made since. Nonetheless,&nbsp;
the movie-to-be sits at the very top of Ian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.960
McKellen's IMDb page - which cites the&nbsp;
film as still being in pre-production.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.440
According to Variety, Hamlet Revenant is&nbsp;
an "original adaptation" of the legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.880
Shakespeare play. McKellen's role is unconfirmed&nbsp;
so far, but Connie Nielsen of Wonder Woman fame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:14.160
is signed on as Gertrude, alongside Mikkel Boe&nbsp;
Følsgaard as the titular angst-ridden prince.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.720
With any luck, Hamlet Revenant will come together&nbsp;
sometime soon. Hollywood hasn't really committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.680
to Shakespeare since the '90s, and the world is&nbsp;
long overdue for a few new spins on the Bard's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.400
classics. And who better to lead the charge than&nbsp;
one of the great Shakespearean actors of his time?

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.640
The character of Hamlet is generally&nbsp;
depicted as a younger man; in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.120
he's occasionally cited as a straight-up metaphor&nbsp;
for adolescence. But is that going to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:41.840
Ian McKellen from picking up poor Yorick's&nbsp;
skull and giving the role his all? Heck no.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.600
Originally delayed by the COVID-19&nbsp;
lockdown, the Theatre Royal Windsor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.480
production of Hamlet is scheduled to&nbsp;
run from June to September of 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:55.680
which should keep McKellen, in his capacity as&nbsp;
the titular depressed prince, very busy indeed.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:59.440
Of course, you might argue that McKellen's&nbsp;
age might make Hamlet's youthful struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:03.680
a little more difficult to believe. Then again,&nbsp;
it could be that his age will do wonders for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:08.960
kind of existential dread that's so crucial for&nbsp;
a really great performance of the infamous Dane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.520
Whatever the case, McKellen sounded optimistic&nbsp;
in the show's press release, in which he said:

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:16.160
"How can Hamlet be played&nbsp;
by an 80 year old? I hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.480
theatre-starved audiences will want to find out!"

